    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "67012ee15d8884ae85f9776dbde1fdd9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1513,
    "chunk_text": "As you know, this particular cemetery has been in use since 1970s and when they were trying to establish an electrical cremation facility, they uncovered those human remains. On the 19th of February, the Jaffna Police reported the discovery of the suspected human remains to the Magistrate's Court of Jaffna after conducting initial investigations and recording statements from the cemetery maintenance staff and committee members.\n\n2393 2394\n\n\u0db4\u0dcf\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dbd\u0dd2\u0dc1\u0db8\u0dca\u0db1\u0dca\u0dad\u0dd4\u0dc0\n\nAlso, on the 20th of February, 2025, a joint site inspection was carried out by the Jaffna Police's Crime Division with the Magistrate, the Judicial Medical Officer, the Consultant Forensic Pathologist and the officers from my own institution, the Office on Missing Persons. On the same day, the JMO collected samples from the site for preliminary forensic examinations and submitted an anthropological report to the Magistrate's Court. The report confirmed that except for two samples, all collected bones were of humans.\n\nAs per the expert opinion, the JMO recommended a PIT method to be adopted. Initially, a budget was drafted and we already allocated Rs. 2.1 million on the 21st of April, 2025. In this site, 19 sets of human remains were discovered, exhumed and preserved as court exhibits. Based on these observations, the JMO informed the Court that further excavation is needed. On the 13th of June, the High Court of Jaffna has sent us a request for a further budget, which is now in the process of being allocated."
  },
