    "md5": "05bdd066764a4059230cb52dee9118ee",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1428,
    "chunk_text": "As per the expert opinion, the JMO recommended a PIT method to be adopted. Initially, a budget was drafted and we already allocated Rs. 2.1 million on the 21st of April, 2025. In this site, 19 sets of human remains were discovered, exhumed and preserved as court exhibits. Based on these observations, the JMO informed the Court that further excavation is needed. On the 13th of June, the High Court of Jaffna has sent us a request for a further budget, which is now in the process of being allocated.\n\n2. Then, you asked about the Kokkuthoduvai grave site. On the 29th of June, 2023, the site was discovered when the National Water Supply and Drainage Board was digging the area to lay water pipes. It has been reported under No. AR/804/23 to the Court. The human remains had been carefully removed from the site by experts and sent to the Forensic Medicine Department of the Medical Faculty of the University of Jaffna. Now, the reports by the experts had been submitted to the Magistrate's Court of Mullaithivu awaiting it to\n\ntake the next steps. The experts had ensured that there are no human remains left in the site; everything had carefully been removed and handed over to the authorities.\n\nMore than Rs. 15 million had been requested by the JMO of Mullaitivu and that was allocated by our Ministry. The Court has to decide on the future steps, which we will support.\n\n3 &4. Hon. Speaker, the Mandaitivu mass grave is a"
