  {
    "doc_type": "lk_hansard_2020s",
    "doc_id": "2025-06-17-2025-06-17",
    "num": "2025-06-17",
    "date_str": "2025-06-17",
    "description": "Hansard of June 17, 2025",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1750929357043199.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-06-17-2025-06-17-0055",
    "chunk_index": 55,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "ef4102b74a3f5e9b6eb1355aedfa40b6",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1774,
    "chunk_text": "More than Rs. 15 million had been requested by the JMO of Mullaitivu and that was allocated by our Ministry. The Court has to decide on the future steps, which we will support.\n\n3 &4. Hon. Speaker, the Mandaitivu mass grave is a\n\n5. The Thiruketheeswaran grave was found in Mannar and its Case No. is, B 768/2013. Human remains were carefully removed from the site by an expert team and as per the court order, the skeletal remain are kept under the custody of Judicial Medical Officer for further forensic medical examination. Requests have been made by the JMO to send the samples for analysis, for radio-carbon dating. The JMO has also requested for US Dollars 3,875 and recommended that the samples are sent to Florida, USA for carbon analysis. The budget for radio-carbon testing of bone samples was prepared by the courts - we do not prepare the budget; the court tells us the amount required - and was sent to the Ministry of Justice, which had subsequently been approved. So, that is the status with regard to that matter.\n\n2395 2396\n\n[\u0d9c\u0dbb\u0dd4 \u0db1\u0dd3\u0dd2 \u0dad \u0dc4\u0dbb\u0dca\u0dc2\u0dca\u0da8\u0da7 \u0db1\u0dcf\u0db1\u0dcf\u0dba\u0d9a\u0dca\u0d9a\u0dcf\u0dba \u0db8\u0dc4\u0dad\u0dcf]\n\n2025 \u0da2\u0dd4\u0db1\u0dd2 17\n\nAnd, further, the court has instructed to adopt an international procurement policy for sending the samples through a third party while the chain of custody is ensured. We have to make sure that these samples do not get mixed up, planted or changed because at the end of the day, we are here to do justice and for that, you need to find out the culprits. So, we do not need any allegations on that part. The Magistrate's Court has to give us further orders. It was highlighted that the entire process of sending samples for carbon-dating should be conducted in a transparent manner with accountability at all stages."
  },
