    "chunk_text": "Sir, we must not wait for this issue to spiral into a national health crisis. If we fail, we would be condemning an entire generation for a future of chronic illnesses, psychological trauma and broken dreams. This is not merely a sports issue, but a matter of our national health, of youth development and therefore, warrants our collective moral responsibility.\n\n2487 2488\n\n2025 \u0da2\u0dd4\u0db1\u0dd2 17\n\npeace, justice and protection of the innocents, whether on our soil or abroad. That, Sir, is true sportsmanship. To be silent is complicity. Let this be a Parliament that upholds human dignity and values in all its forms from the playing fields to the battlefields, from our gyms and stadiums to the war-torn streets of Gaza. Let our message be loud and clear: stop the violence! We, as Sri Lankans, call upon all parties to abide by the moral obligation of the two-State solution as presented by the United Nations with a sovereign Palestine existing side by side with Israel based on the 1967 borders.\n\nSir, let us be proactive, let us legislate not with the intent to punish but to protect and for the sake of peace.\n\nThere is one more thing I would like to add, Sir, as we talk of banned substances in sports. We have to look at the gyms that we have today. I have a knowledge of a few of the gyms where youngsters gather. We should pay more attention to what is happening there. Those gyms are unregulated and are not registered with any authority. At those gyms, youngsters gather and I am of the knowledge that in some, narcotic substances are freely exchanged. Nobody contests or asks them what is happening there because people feel that it is a place where people come to maintain their fitness and good health. Narcotic substances are being traded in some of those gyms. That is another reason why gyms should be regulated, registered and supervised.\n\nLet us inculcate in our youth the tools to grow strong, not just in body and mind, but in discipline and most of all, in moral values."
