    "url_metadata": "https://www.parliament.lk/en/business-of-parliament/hansards",
    "lang": "si-ta-en",
    "url_pdf": "https://www.parliament.lk/uploads/documents/hansard/1754386160089643.pdf",
    "chunk_id": "2025-07-23-2025-07-23-0171",
    "chunk_index": 171,
    "language": "si-ta-en",
    "md5": "45f5bb4d7caadd3327194f0fcc7edb72",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1866,
    "chunk_text": "Madam, today, we are debating Amendments to the Companies Act. I was listening to many speakers. Amendments to the Companies Act are being brought in to ensure prevention of money laundering, prevention of black money and with all of those intentions. I think 20 odd Sections of the Act are being amended and those are things that we welcome. But, I noticed everybody else except the Minister repeatedly concentrating on the notion of preventing terrorism financing and how important these Amendments are towards that.\n\nLast week, people from Valikamam North, particularly from a village called Myliddy, came down to Colombo and protested in front of the Presidential Secretariat and some of them went inside and met with, I think, one of the Secretaries or the Private Secretary to the President. They were protesting, Madam, because in 2013, 6,317 acres were gazetted under the Land Acquisition Act for military purposes, for High Security Zones. Then, eventually, because of international pressure and protests of the Tamil people, almost 3,000 acres were released and now, there are 3,000 acres that are still not released. The Secretary to the Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock, Land and Irrigation, we have found out, is thinking in terms of gazetting those remaining 3,000 acres as a High Security Zone permanently, refusing to allow them to be released. That is against your own policy; that is against the promises you gave! Today, out of these 3,000 acres, Madam, almost 75 per cent is not used for military purposes; it is used for farming by the Army, the Navy and the Air Force, for you all to bring vegetables to the local market and undercut the local farmer. Surely, is it not something that your Government should be against? You should be on the side of the ordinary people. Over 75 per cent of the lands are used for non-military purposes, Madam."
  },
